# Copy to bot/.env and fill in. That copy is gitignored (**/.env at any depth)
# and is never deployed -- it is not in the repository Railway clones.
#
# ON RAILWAY, set these same names as service variables, under the service's
# Settings -> Variables. lib/settings.py reads the real environment first and
# falls back to bot/.env, so one set of names works in both places and nothing
# has to branch on where it is running.
#
# A NEW NAME MUST BE ADDED TO KNOWN_KEYS in lib/settings.py. The environment is
# overlaid by name, and there is no .env file on Railway to learn the names
# from, so a key missing from that list is read locally and ignored in
# production -- silently, because nothing knows it was meant to be there.
#
# Which component needs what:
#
#   MISTRAL_API_KEY      the app, and both scripts
#   AGENT_ID             the app, and both scripts
#   MISTRAL_LIBRARY_ID   bot/scripts/configure_agent.py only -- the app never
#                        reads it, because the library is attached to the agent
#                        rather than queried directly
#   DATABASE_URL         the app, and both scripts that touch it -- set_users.py
#                        and read_feedback.py

# --- Mistral ---------------------------------------------------------------
# The library and the agent are created once in the Mistral console;
# bot/scripts/configure_agent.py fills the library with the generated knowledge
# and points the agent at it.
MISTRAL_API_KEY=
AGENT_ID=
MISTRAL_LIBRARY_ID=

# --- Storage ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Accounts and feedback.
#
# On Railway set this to the reference ${{Postgres.DATABASE_URL}} rather than a
# pasted string, so it follows the database service. It must be Postgres there:
# container filesystems are ephemeral, so a SQLite file would be discarded on
# every deploy, taking the accounts with it.
#
# On your own machine there are two ways to run, and the choice is really about
# what "local" means here:
#
#   Leave it unset. lib/db.py falls back to a SQLite file at bot/local.db, so
#   the app runs without provisioning anything and nothing you do can touch a
#   live account. Reach production for a single command by putting the variable
#   in front of it, which sets it for that one process only:
#
#       DATABASE_URL="<public url>" python bot/scripts/set_users.py
#
#   Or set it here, to Railway's *public* Postgres URL -- the internal hostname
#   only resolves inside their network. Then this machine administers
#   production: set_users.py rewrites the live accounts, and the app run locally
#   reads and writes the live tables. There is no sandbox while it is set.
#
# This deployment does the second, there being one administrator and one copy
# of bot/users.txt. It is a real choice rather than a default: the cost is that
# a stale users.txt run against production revokes everyone missing from it.
DATABASE_URL=
